           <p>The Federal Open Market Committee raised its target range by one-quarter percentage point to {decision.range.lower}–{decision.range.upper.toFixed(2)} percent, effective September 17.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point" /><Cite s="fomc-implementation-2026-09-16" passage="effective September 17, 2026" /></p>
         </SourcedBlock>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">It was the first increase since July 27, 2023.<Cite s="fed-open-market-operations-2026-09-16" passage="September 17 | 25 | 0 | 3.75-4.00" /><Cite s="fed-open-market-operations-2026-09-16" passage="July 27 | 25 | 0 | 5.25-5.50" /></p>
 
         <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
-          <figcaption><strong>The first increase since 2023</strong><span>Upper limit of the target range</span></figcaption>
+          <figcaption><strong>The target range turns higher</strong><span>Upper limit of the federal funds target range</span></figcaption>
           <StepChart
             {...ratePath}
             yRange={[3.5, 5.75]}
             annotations={[
               { date: "2024-01-01", value: 5.5, text: "5.50%", accent: false },
               { date: "2026-09-19", value: 4, text: "4.00%", anchor: "end", accent: true },
             ]}
-            caption="The upper bound fell in six steps from September 2024 through December 2025, then rose a quarter point effective September 17, 2026."
+            caption="Upper limit of the federal funds target range, January 2024 to September 19, 2026."
             ariaLabel="Federal funds target range upper limit from January 2024 through September 19, 2026"
           />
           <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Computed from {seriesReceipt.rangeCount.toLocaleString("en-US")} daily DFEDTARU observations saved September 19.</p>
         </figure>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">Seven weeks earlier, most policymakers had wanted more information before acting, though several already favored an increase.<Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="Several participants favored an increase of 25 basis points" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh had set a public test in late August: inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal “clearly and at sufficient speed.” By September, consumer prices were still 3.4 percent above a year earlier, and gasoline had supplied more than one-third of August's monthly increase.<Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="clearly and at sufficient speed" /><Cite s="abc-fed-preview-2026-09-16" passage="Prices rose 3.4% in August compared to a year earlier" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh had set a public test in late August: inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal “clearly and at sufficient speed.” By September, the Consumer Price Index was {inflation.augustCpiAnnual} percent above a year earlier, and gasoline had supplied more than one-third of August's monthly increase.<Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="clearly and at sufficient speed" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="all items index increased 3.4 percent" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Headline economic growth slowed to {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent. Beneath that slowdown, consumer spending and private fixed investment grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent, a measure of domestic demand.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed investment, increased 4.2 percent" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Real gross domestic product grew at a {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent annual rate in the second quarter, down from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent in the first quarter. Consumer spending plus private fixed investment grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent in the second quarter, faster than headline growth.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent in the second quarter of 2026" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="In the first quarter, real GDP increased 2.1 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed investment, increased 4.2 percent in the second quarter" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh gave three reasons for acting: a stronger economy, inflation trends that still failed his test and a changed view of the most and least likely geopolitical outcomes. By September, officials were no longer counting on the Iran-war energy shock being temporary, according to the Associated Press.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="three things that have happened in that intermeeting period" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="our judgment about what is the most likely, or least likely of the geopolitical situation has changed" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="Fed officials are no longer counting on it being a temporary shock" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh pointed to a stronger economy and inflation trends that still failed his test. By September, officials were no longer counting on the energy shock from the war in Iran being temporary, according to the Associated Press; the war had reduced oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="the economy has strengthened" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="inflation summer trends weren't passing the test" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="Fed officials are no longer counting on it being a temporary shock" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="reduction in shipments of oil and natural gas through the Strait of Hormuz" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">The wording also hardened. July attributed part of the inflation problem to supply shocks in sectors including energy. September dropped that explanation and said tighter policy would support a “timelier return” to 2 percent.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="Today's policy action will support a timelier return" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">The wording also hardened. In July, the committee attributed part of the inflation problem to supply shocks in sectors including energy. In September, the committee dropped that explanation and said tighter policy would support a “timelier return” to 2 percent.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="Today's policy action will support a timelier return" /></p>
       </section>
 
       <section id="what-rates-can-change">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The mechanism</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">A higher overnight rate makes borrowing more expensive and slows consumer and business spending. That reduces demand across the economy.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" /></p>
 
         <SourcedBlock source="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" kind="record">
           <p>That tool leaves the price of oil and gasoline untouched. Warsh's concern was the next step: the shock broadening into other prices and inflation expectations.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We cannot affect any individual price" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="relative prices don't broaden out" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="inflation expectations remain well-anchored" /></p>
         </SourcedBlock>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Lower oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz, disrupted refining and Red Sea shipping had already raised energy and transport costs. CBO estimated that energy added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized second-quarter increase in the Fed's preferred household-price measure. Prices outside food and energy were expected to stay elevated longer because the higher transport costs reached other goods and services more slowly.<Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="reduction in shipments of oil and natural gas through the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized rate of inflation" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="higher energy prices take longer to affect prices of nonenergy goods and services" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Disrupted refining and Red Sea shipping added to the energy and transport costs. The Congressional Budget Office estimated that energy added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized second-quarter PCE inflation rate. CBO expected core PCE inflation, which excludes food and energy, to remain elevated longer because higher energy prices reach nonenergy goods and services more slowly.<Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="conflict has disrupted global refining activities" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized rate of inflation" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="core PCE inflation will remain elevated longer" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="core PCE price index excludes prices for food and energy" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="higher energy prices take longer to affect prices of nonenergy goods and services" /></p>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">Interest-rate changes reach spending, jobs and prices with a delay. The energy shock could fade before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" /></p>
       </section>
 
       <section id="who-feels-it">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Borrowers and savers</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>
 
         <SourcedBlock source="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
           <p>Credit-card rates can respond quickly because most cards carry variable rates tied to banks' prime rate. Auto loans and adjustable-rate mortgages can also become more expensive, while savings accounts and certificates of deposit may pay more.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Fed's policy changes flow through to credit card rates rapidly" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="interest rates on savings accounts and certificates of deposit are headed higher" /></p>
         </SourcedBlock>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">Fixed mortgage rates tend to follow the 10-year Treasury yield. Existing fixed-rate loans keep the rate in their contract.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="many homeowners locked in low mortgage rates" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz expected one quarter-point increase to have a small effect. The burden grows if several increases stack up over time.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz expected one quarter-point increase to have a small effect. He said several increases would matter more as they stacked up over time.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh said persistent inflation falls especially hard on people who live from paycheck to paycheck without financial assets, home equity or retirement-plan equity. Stable prices protect the buying power of their take-home pay.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="people that don't own financial assets" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="they're living off their paycheck" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="deliver real take home pay increases" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh said people without financial assets, home equity or retirement-plan equity live on paychecks that arrive every few weeks. With inflation at the Fed's 2 percent objective, he said, their wages could “deliver real take home pay increases.”<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="people that don't own financial assets" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="they're living off their paycheck that comes every couple of weeks" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="deliver real take home pay increases" /></p>
       </section>
 
       <section id="projections">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The signal beyond the vote</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">The projections</h2>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Sixteen of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected at least one more quarter-point increase by the end of 2026. Their median year-end rate rose from {projections.june2026Median} percent in June to {projections.september2026Median} percent in September.<Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection          3.8    3.6" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Sixteen of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected at least one more quarter-point increase by the end of 2026. Their median year-end rate rose from {projections.june2026Median} percent in June to {projections.september2026Median} percent in September. The 2027 median was also {projections.september2027Median} percent.<Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection          3.8    3.6" /></p>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">Each individual projection gives where one policymaker thought the rate should stand at year-end. Warsh submitted no projection and declined to guide the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual participant’s judgment of the midpoint of the appropriate target range" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I have not offered a projection of" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="My business is to not give forward guidance" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Policymakers projected inflation in the Fed's preferred household-price measure at 3.7 percent for 2026, up from 3.6 percent in June, and 2.3 percent for 2027. Their median reaches 2 percent in 2029.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="PCE inflation              3.7    2.3   2.1   2.0" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection           3.6    2.3   2.0" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">The Consumer Price Index was {inflation.augustCpiAnnual} percent above a year earlier in August. The Fed's preferred measure, the PCE price index, was {inflation.julyPceAnnual} percent above a year earlier in July. Policymakers projected a 3.7 percent rise in PCE prices from the end of 2025 to the end of 2026, up from their 3.6 percent June projection, and 2.3 percent for 2027. Their median reaches 2 percent in 2029.<Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="all items index increased 3.4 percent" /><Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="The Fed's preferred measure of inflation, the 12-month change in the PCE price index" /><Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="PCE price index for July increased 3.7 percent" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="PCE inflation              3.7    2.3   2.1   2.0" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection           3.6    2.3   2.0" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="percent changes from the fourth quarter of the previous year to the fourth quarter of the year indicated" /></p>
       </section>
 
       <section id="the-catch">
         <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
           <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
           <ol>
             <li>
-              <h3>The matching 4.1 percent medians conceal different paths</h3>
-              CBS wrote that “policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027.” Each year's 4.1 percent figure is the middle of a separate set of individual year-end judgments. It does not establish a committee decision to hold at every 2027 meeting.<Cite s="cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="For each period, the median is the middle projection" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="appropriate target level for the federal funds rate at the end of the specified calendar year" />
+              <h3>Each 4.1 percent median describes its own year</h3>
+              CBS wrote that “policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027.” The two figures are separate midpoints of officials' individual year-end judgments, one for 2026 and one for 2027.<Cite s="cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="For each period, the median is the middle projection" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="appropriate target level for the federal funds rate at the end of the specified calendar year" />
             </li>
           </ol>
         </aside>
       </section>
 
       <section id="political-pressure">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The White House and the central bank</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">The pressure around the decision</h2>
 
         <SourcedBlock source="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
           <p>About two hours after the announcement, President Donald Trump demanded rates of 1 percent or less and called for the Fed to lower them fast.<Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" /><Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Trump’s reaction came about two hours" /></p>
         </SourcedBlock>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump said the next morning that he had told Warsh to vote with the board because the result would not change. He called it “a very tough board” and “very hostile.”<Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="I talked to Kevin, and I said, ‘You might as well vote with the board because it’s not going to matter.’" /><Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="a very tough board" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump portrayed Warsh as unable to change the result, saying he had told the chair to cast the same vote as the rest of the board. He called it “a very tough board” and “very hostile.”<Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="I talked to Kevin, and I said, ‘You might as well vote with the board because it’s not going to matter.’" /><Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="a very tough board" /></p>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh described central-bank independence as “a two-way street” and said trade and fiscal policymakers should stay in their lane.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Independence is a two-way street" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="trade policy and fiscal policy stay in their lane too" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">The pressure's effect on the vote remains unresolved. All 12 voters backed the increase, and Warsh declined to discuss conversations with Trump.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I don't have anything for you on discussions with the president" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">All 12 voters backed the increase, and Warsh declined to discuss conversations with Trump.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I don't have anything for you on discussions with the president" /></p>
 
         <p data-layer="narrative">The dispute was also about what a supply shock calls for. Before the meeting, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent argued that the Fed should wait for a shock to spread through the economy before raising rates.<Cite s="dow-jones-bessent-rate-hike-2026-08-31" passage="traditionally you don't raise [rates]...unless you see second or third-order effects" /></p>
       </section>
 
       <section id="what-happened-next">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 17 and 18</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
 
         <SourcedBlock source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" kind="checked">
           <p>The 10-year Treasury yield slipped the next day, a move the Associated Press tied to easing inflation worries. That yield strongly influences mortgage rates, while the Fed controls a short-term rate.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit, a likely sign of reduced inflation worries" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="The 10-year Treasury, for instance, strongly influences mortgage rates" /></p>
         </SourcedBlock>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, market-implied odds of another increase in October had risen to about 58 percent from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, while Bank of America expected increases in October and December. Natixis strategist Jack Janasiewicz instead saw the move as a reversal of the “insurance cuts” from 2025 rather than the start of an aggressive tightening cycle.<Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="near 58% Friday morning" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="Jack Janasiewicz, portfolio manager and lead portfolio strategist" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="removal of the insurance cuts the Fed delivered in the fall of 2025" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, CME Group's FedWatch futures-market gauge put the odds of another increase in October at about 58 percent, up from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, while Bank of America expected increases in October and December. Natixis strategist Jack Janasiewicz said, “We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle.”<Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="near 58% Friday morning, according to the CME Group's FedWatch gauge" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="A week ago, the probability was 42%" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="Jack Janasiewicz, portfolio manager and lead portfolio strategist" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle" /></p>
 
         <SourcedBlock source="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
           <DataTable columns={["When", "Record"]} rows={chronologyRows} caption="Decision chronology" />
         </SourcedBlock>
       </section>
 
       <section id="unknowns">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
         <ul data-layer="fact">
           <li>Whether another increase follows this one. The year-end views are individual judgments, and Warsh declined to prejudge the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" /></li>
           <li>How long the Iran-war energy shock lasts.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="the gas price shock could be over" /></li>
           <li>What the next inflation and growth reports show. August inflation in the Fed's preferred measure and the annual national-account updates, including GDP revisions, are due September 30.<Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="Next release: September 30, 2026" /><Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="annual updates of national, industry, and regional data" /></li>
           <li>What the September deliberations add. The minutes are due {decision.minutesDueLabel}, three weeks after the decision.<Cite s="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" passage="The minutes of regularly scheduled meetings are released three weeks after the date of the policy decision." /></li>
         </ul>
       </section>
 
       <StorySources event={event.slug} />
       <StoryState view={state} supporting />
     </main>
   </div>
 </BaseLayout>}
 
 <style is:global>
   .sourced-block { margin: 1rem 0; }
   .sourced-block > p { margin: 0; }
   .sourced-block > .chip { display: none; }
   .mode-work .sourced-block > .chip { display: inline-block; }
   .mode-work .sourced-block { border-left: 3px solid var(--blue); padding-left: 1rem; }
   .event-record-context { margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
   #records .section-lede { display: none; }
   .src-ref { font-size: 0.72em; line-height: 1.15; margin-left: 0.15rem; }
   .src-ref a { color: var(--muted, #6b7280); text-decoration: none; }
   .src-ref a:hover { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: underline; }
 </style>
