    55	      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>
    56	
    57	      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
    58	        <p class="sources-line">The proof layer holds the September statement, implementation note, projections and preliminary press-conference transcript; the July statement and minutes; the official rate history; the policy framework and Jackson Hole speech; economic releases; and the coverage checked against them. The CBO page was recovered from a September 16 Internet Archive copy after its direct fetch returned 403. The Wall Street Journal live card is preserved through its Dow Jones Newswires syndication. The Associated Press reconstruction is preserved as registry-extracted text and markdown because its run retained no raw-body receipt.</p>
    59	      </div>
    60	
    61	      <section id="what-happened">
    62	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The decision</p>
    63	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
    64	
    65	        <p data-layer="narrative">CME Group's FedWatch futures-market gauge put {decision.preDecisionHikeOdds} percent odds on a quarter-point increase the day before the decision. Markets expected the increase; the committee's {decision.julyVote.forHold}–{decision.julyVote.forIncrease} hold in July became a {event.kpis[1].value} increase in September.<Cite s="abc-fed-preview-2026-09-16" passage="odds of a quarter-point rate hike stood at 94%" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote" /></p>
    66	
    67	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" kind="record">
    68	          <p>The Federal Open Market Committee raised its target range by one-quarter percentage point to {decision.range.lower}–{decision.range.upper.toFixed(2)} percent, effective September 17.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point" /><Cite s="fomc-implementation-2026-09-16" passage="effective September 17, 2026" /></p>
    69	        </SourcedBlock>
    70	
    71	        <p data-layer="narrative">It was the first increase since July 27, 2023.<Cite s="fed-open-market-operations-2026-09-16" passage="September 17 | 25 | 0 | 3.75-4.00" /><Cite s="fed-open-market-operations-2026-09-16" passage="July 27 | 25 | 0 | 5.25-5.50" /></p>
    72	
    73	        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
    74	          <figcaption><strong>The target range turns higher</strong><span>Upper limit of the federal funds target range</span></figcaption>
    75	          <StepChart
    76	            {...ratePath}
    77	            yRange={[3.5, 5.75]}
    78	            annotations={[
    79	              { date: "2024-01-01", value: 5.5, text: "5.50%", accent: false },
    80	              { date: "2026-09-19", value: 4, text: "4.00%", anchor: "end", accent: true },
    81	            ]}
    82	            caption="Upper limit of the federal funds target range, January 2024 to September 19, 2026."
    83	            ariaLabel="Federal funds target range upper limit from January 2024 through September 19, 2026"
    84	          />
    85	          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Computed from {seriesReceipt.rangeCount.toLocaleString("en-US")} daily DFEDTARU observations saved September 19.</p>
    86	        </figure>
    87	
    88	        <p data-layer="narrative">Seven weeks earlier, most policymakers had wanted more information before acting, though several already favored an increase.<Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="Several participants favored an increase of 25 basis points" /></p>
    89	
    90	        <p data-layer="narrative">Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh had set a public test in late August: inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal “clearly and at sufficient speed.” By September, the Consumer Price Index was {inflation.augustCpiAnnual} percent above a year earlier, and gasoline had supplied more than one-third of August's monthly increase.<Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="clearly and at sufficient speed" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="all items index increased 3.4 percent" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /></p>
    91	
    92	        <p data-layer="narrative">Real gross domestic product grew at a {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent annual rate in the second quarter, down from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent in the first quarter. Consumer spending plus private fixed investment grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent in the second quarter, faster than headline growth.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent in the second quarter of 2026" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="In the first quarter, real GDP increased 2.1 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed investment, increased 4.2 percent in the second quarter" /></p>
    93	
    94	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh pointed to a stronger economy and inflation trends that still failed his test. By September, officials were no longer counting on the energy shock from the war in Iran being temporary, according to the Associated Press; the war had reduced oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="the economy has strengthened" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="inflation summer trends weren't passing the test" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="Fed officials are no longer counting on it being a temporary shock" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="reduction in shipments of oil and natural gas through the Strait of Hormuz" /></p>
    95	
    96	        <p data-layer="narrative">The wording also hardened. In July, the committee attributed part of the inflation problem to supply shocks in sectors including energy. In September, the committee dropped that explanation and said tighter policy would support a “timelier return” to 2 percent.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="Today's policy action will support a timelier return" /></p>
    97	      </section>
    98	
    99	      <section id="what-rates-can-change">
   100	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The mechanism</p>
   101	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2>
   102	
   103	        <p data-layer="narrative">A higher overnight rate makes borrowing more expensive and slows consumer and business spending. That reduces demand across the economy.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" /></p>
   104	
   105	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" kind="record">
   106	          <p>That tool leaves the price of oil and gasoline untouched. Warsh's concern was the next step: the shock broadening into other prices and inflation expectations.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We cannot affect any individual price" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="relative prices don't broaden out" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="inflation expectations remain well-anchored" /></p>
   107	        </SourcedBlock>
   108	
   109	        <p data-layer="narrative">Disrupted refining and Red Sea shipping added to the energy and transport costs. The Congressional Budget Office estimated that energy added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized second-quarter PCE inflation rate. CBO expected core PCE inflation, which excludes food and energy, to remain elevated longer because higher energy prices reach nonenergy goods and services more slowly.<Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="conflict has disrupted global refining activities" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized rate of inflation" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="core PCE inflation will remain elevated longer" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="core PCE price index excludes prices for food and energy" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="higher energy prices take longer to affect prices of nonenergy goods and services" /></p>
   110	
   111	        <p data-layer="narrative">Interest-rate changes reach spending, jobs and prices with a delay. The energy shock could fade before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" /></p>
   112	      </section>
   113	
   114	      <section id="who-feels-it">
   115	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Borrowers and savers</p>
   116	        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>
   117	
   118	        <SourcedBlock source="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
   119	          <p>Credit-card rates can respond quickly because most cards carry variable rates tied to banks' prime rate. Auto loans and adjustable-rate mortgages can also become more expensive, while savings accounts and certificates of deposit may pay more.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Fed's policy changes flow through to credit card rates rapidly" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="interest rates on savings accounts and certificates of deposit are headed higher" /></p>
   120	        </SourcedBlock>
   121	
   122	        <p data-layer="narrative">Fixed mortgage rates tend to follow the 10-year Treasury yield. Existing fixed-rate loans keep the rate in their contract.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="many homeowners locked in low mortgage rates" /></p>
   123	
   124	        <p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz expected one quarter-point increase to have a small effect. He said several increases would matter more as they stacked up over time.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /></p>
   125	
   126	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh said people without financial assets, home equity or retirement-plan equity live on paychecks that arrive every few weeks. With inflation at the Fed's 2 percent objective, he said, their wages could “deliver real take home pay increases.”<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="people that don't own financial assets" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="they're living off their paycheck that comes every couple of weeks" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="deliver real take home pay increases" /></p>
   127	      </section>
   128	
   129	      <section id="projections">
   130	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The signal beyond the vote</p>
   131	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The projections</h2>
   132	
   133	        <p data-layer="narrative">Sixteen of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected at least one more quarter-point increase by the end of 2026. Their median year-end rate rose from {projections.june2026Median} percent in June to {projections.september2026Median} percent in September. The 2027 median was also {projections.september2027Median} percent.<Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection          3.8    3.6" /></p>
   134	
   135	        <p data-layer="narrative">Each individual projection gives where one policymaker thought the rate should stand at year-end. Warsh submitted no projection and declined to guide the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual participant’s judgment of the midpoint of the appropriate target range" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I have not offered a projection of" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="My business is to not give forward guidance" /></p>
   136	
   137	        <p data-layer="narrative">The Consumer Price Index was {inflation.augustCpiAnnual} percent above a year earlier in August. The Fed's preferred measure, the PCE price index, was {inflation.julyPceAnnual} percent above a year earlier in July. Policymakers projected a 3.7 percent rise in PCE prices from the end of 2025 to the end of 2026, up from their 3.6 percent June projection, and 2.3 percent for 2027. Their median reaches 2 percent in 2029.<Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="all items index increased 3.4 percent" /><Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="The Fed's preferred measure of inflation, the 12-month change in the PCE price index" /><Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="PCE price index for July increased 3.7 percent" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="PCE inflation              3.7    2.3   2.1   2.0" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection           3.6    2.3   2.0" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="percent changes from the fourth quarter of the previous year to the fourth quarter of the year indicated" /></p>
   138	      </section>
   139	
   140	      <section id="the-catch">
   141	        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
   142	          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
   143	          <ol>
   144	            <li>
   145	              <h3>Each 4.1 percent median describes its own year</h3>
   146	              CBS wrote that “policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027.” The two figures are separate midpoints of officials' individual year-end judgments, one for 2026 and one for 2027.<Cite s="cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="For each period, the median is the middle projection" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="appropriate target level for the federal funds rate at the end of the specified calendar year" />
   147	            </li>
   148	          </ol>
   149	        </aside>
   150	      </section>
   151	
   152	      <section id="political-pressure">
   153	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The White House and the central bank</p>
   154	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The pressure around the decision</h2>
   155	
   156	        <SourcedBlock source="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
   157	          <p>About two hours after the announcement, President Donald Trump demanded rates of 1 percent or less and called for the Fed to lower them fast.<Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" /><Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Trump’s reaction came about two hours" /></p>
   158	        </SourcedBlock>
   159	
   160	        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump portrayed Warsh as unable to change the result, saying he had told the chair to cast the same vote as the rest of the board. He called it “a very tough board” and “very hostile.”<Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="I talked to Kevin, and I said, ‘You might as well vote with the board because it’s not going to matter.’" /><Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="a very tough board" /></p>
   161	
   162	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh described central-bank independence as “a two-way street” and said trade and fiscal policymakers should stay in their lane.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Independence is a two-way street" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="trade policy and fiscal policy stay in their lane too" /></p>
   163	
   164	        <p data-layer="narrative">All 12 voters backed the increase, and Warsh declined to discuss conversations with Trump.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I don't have anything for you on discussions with the president" /></p>
   165	
   166	        <p data-layer="narrative">The dispute was also about what a supply shock calls for. Before the meeting, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent argued that the Fed should wait for a shock to spread through the economy before raising rates.<Cite s="dow-jones-bessent-rate-hike-2026-08-31" passage="traditionally you don't raise [rates]...unless you see second or third-order effects" /></p>
   167	      </section>
   168	
   169	      <section id="what-happened-next">
   170	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 17 and 18</p>
   171	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
   172	
   173	        <SourcedBlock source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" kind="checked">
   174	          <p>The 10-year Treasury yield slipped the next day, a move the Associated Press tied to easing inflation worries. That yield strongly influences mortgage rates, while the Fed controls a short-term rate.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit, a likely sign of reduced inflation worries" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="The 10-year Treasury, for instance, strongly influences mortgage rates" /></p>
   175	        </SourcedBlock>
   176	
   177	        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, CME Group's FedWatch futures-market gauge put the odds of another increase in October at about 58 percent, up from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, while Bank of America expected increases in October and December. Natixis strategist Jack Janasiewicz said, “We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle.”<Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="near 58% Friday morning, according to the CME Group's FedWatch gauge" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="A week ago, the probability was 42%" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="Jack Janasiewicz, portfolio manager and lead portfolio strategist" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle" /></p>
   178	
   179	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
   180	          <DataTable columns={["When", "Record"]} rows={chronologyRows} caption="Decision chronology" />
   181	        </SourcedBlock>
   182	      </section>
   183	
   184	      <section id="unknowns">
   185	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
   186	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
   187	        <ul data-layer="fact">
   188	          <li>Whether another increase follows this one. The year-end views are individual judgments, and Warsh declined to prejudge the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" /></li>
   189	          <li>How long the Iran-war energy shock lasts.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="the gas price shock could be over" /></li>
   190	          <li>What the next inflation and growth reports show. August inflation in the Fed's preferred measure and the annual national-account updates, including GDP revisions, are due September 30.<Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="Next release: September 30, 2026" /><Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="annual updates of national, industry, and regional data" /></li>
