    74 The Consumer Price Index news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Wednesday, October 14, 2026,
    75 at 8:30 a.m. (ET).
    76 Technical Note
    77 Brief Explanation of the CPI
    78 The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures the change in prices paid by consumers for goods and services. The CPI reflects
    79 spending patterns for each of two population groups: all urban consumers and urban wage earners and clerical workers.
    80 The all urban consumer group represents over 90 percent of the total U.S. population. It is based on the expenditures
    81 of almost all residents of urban or metropolitan areas, including professionals, the self-employed, the poor, the
    82 unemployed, and retired people, as well as urban wage earners and clerical workers. Not included in the CPI are the
    83 spending patterns of people living in rural nonmetropolitan areas, farming families, people in the Armed Forces, and
    84 those in institutions, such as prisons and mental hospitals. Consumer inflation for all urban consumers is measured by
    85 two indexes, namely, the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U) and the Chained Consumer Price Index for
    86 All Urban Consumers (C-CPI-U).
    87 The Consumer Price Index for Urban Wage Earners and Clerical Workers (CPI-W) is based on the expenditures of households
    88 included in the CPI-U definition that meet two requirements: more than one-half of the household's income must come
    89 from clerical or wage occupations, and at least one of the household's earners must have been employed for at least 37
    90 weeks during the previous 12 months. The CPI-W population represents approximately 30 percent of the total U.S.
    91 population and is a subset of the CPI-U population.
    92 The CPIs are based on prices of food, clothing, shelter, fuels, transportation, doctors' and dentists' services, drugs,
    93 and other goods and services that people buy for day-to-day living. Prices are collected each month in 75 urban areas
    94 across the country from about 6,000 housing units and approximately 22,000 retail establishments (department stores,
    95 supermarkets, hospitals, and other types of stores and service establishments). All taxes directly associated with the
    96 purchase and use of items are included in the index. Prices of fuels and a few other items are obtained every month in
    97 all 75 locations. Prices of most other commodities and services are collected every month in the three largest
    98 geographic areas and every other month in other areas. Prices of most goods and services are obtained by personal
    99 visit, telephone call, web, or app collection by the Bureau's trained representatives.
   100 In calculating the index, price changes for the various items in each location are aggregated using weights, which
   101 represent their importance in the spending of the appropriate population group. Local data are then combined to obtain
   102 a U.S. city average. For the CPI-U and CPI-W, separate indexes are also published by size of city, by region of the
   103 country, for cross-classifications of regions and population-size classes, and for 23 selected local areas. Area
   104 indexes do not measure differences in the level of prices among cities; they only measure the average change in prices
   105 for each area since the base period. For the C-CPI-U, data are issued only at the national level. The CPI-U and CPI-W
   106 are considered final when released, but the C-CPI-U is issued in preliminary form and subject to three subsequent
   107 quarterly revisions.
   108 The index measures price change from a designed reference date. For most of the CPI-U and the CPI-W, the reference base
   109 is 1982-84 equals 100. The reference base for the C-CPI-U is December 1999 equals 100. An increase of 7 percent from
   110 the reference base, for example, is shown as 107.000. Alternatively, that relationship can also be expressed as the
   111 price of a base period market basket of goods and services rising from $100 to $107.
   112 Sampling Error in the CPI
   113 The CPI is a statistical estimate that is subject to sampling error because it is based upon a sample of retail prices
   114 and not the complete universe of all prices. BLS calculates and publishes estimates of the 1-month, 2-month, 6-month,
   115 and 12-month percent change standard errors annually for the CPI-U. These standard error estimates can be used to
   116 construct confidence intervals for hypothesis testing. For example, the estimated standard error of the 1-month percent
   117 change is 0.04 percent for the U.S. all items CPI. This means that if we repeatedly sample from the universe of all
   118 retail prices using the same methodology, and estimate a percentage change for each sample, then 95 percent of these
   119 estimates will be within 0.08 percent of the 1-month percentage change based on all retail prices. For example, for a
   120 1-month change of 0.2 percent in the all items CPI-U, we are 95 percent confident that the actual percent change based
   121 on all retail prices would fall between 0.12 and 0.28 percent. For the latest data, including information on how to use
   122 the estimates of standard error, see www.bls.gov/cpi/tables/variance-estimates/home.htm.
   123 Calculating Index Changes
   124 Movements of the indexes from 1 month to another are usually expressed as percent changes rather than changes in index
   125 points, because index point changes are affected by the level of the index in relation to its base period, while
   126 percent changes are not. The following table shows an example of using index values to calculate percent changes:
   127 Item A Item B Item C
   128 Year I 112.500 225.000 110.000
   129 Year II 121.500 243.000 128.000
   130 Change in index points 9.000 18.000 18.000
   131 Percent change 9.0/112.500 x 100 = 8.0 18.0/225.000 x 100 = 8.0 18.0/110.000 x 100 = 16.4
   132 Use of Seasonally Adjusted and Unadjusted Data
   133 The Consumer Price Index (CPI) program produces both unadjusted and seasonally adjusted data. Seasonally adjusted data
   134 are computed using seasonal factors derived by the X-13ARIMA-SEATS seasonal adjustment method. These factors are
   135 updated each February, and the new factors are used to revise the previous 5 years of seasonally adjusted data. The
   136 factors are available at www.bls.gov/web/cpi/cpi-seasonal-factors.xlsx. For more information on data revision
   137 scheduling, please see the Seasonal Adjustment questions and answers page at
   138 www.bls.gov/cpi/seasonal-adjustment/questions-and-answers.htm and the Timeline of Seasonal Adjustment Methodological
   139 Changes at www.bls.gov/cpi/seasonal-adjustment/timeline-seasonal-adjustment-methodology-changes.htm.
   140 How to Use Seasonally Adjusted and Unadjusted Data
   141 For analyzing short-term price trends in the economy, seasonally adjusted changes are usually preferred since they
   142 eliminate the effect of changes that normally occur at the same time and in about the same magnitude every year-such as
   143 price movements resulting from weather events, production cycles, model changeovers, holidays, and sales. This allows
   144 data users to focus on changes that are not typical for the time of year.
   145 The unadjusted data are of primary interest to consumers concerned about the prices they actually pay. Unadjusted data
   146 are also used extensively for escalation purposes. Many collective bargaining contract agreements and pension plans,
   147 for example, tie compensation changes to the Consumer Price Index before adjustment for seasonal variation. BLS advises
   148 against the use of seasonally adjusted data in escalation agreements because seasonally adjusted series are revised
   149 annually for five years.
   150 Intervention Analysis
   151 The Bureau of Labor Statistics uses intervention analysis seasonal adjustment (IASA) for some CPI series. Sometimes
   152 extreme values or sharp movements can distort the underlying seasonal pattern of price change. Intervention analysis
   153 seasonal adjustment is a process by which the distortions caused by such unusual events are estimated and removed from
   154 the data prior to calculation of seasonal factors. The resulting seasonal factors, which more accurately represent the
   155 seasonal pattern, are then applied to the unadjusted data.
   156 For example, this procedure was used for the motor fuel series to offset the effects of the 2009 return to normal
   157 pricing after the worldwide economic downturn in 2008. Retaining this outlier data during seasonal factor calculation
   158 would distort the computation of the seasonal portion of the time series data for motor fuel, so it was estimated and
   159 removed from the data prior to seasonal adjustment. Following that, seasonal factors were calculated based on this
   160 "prior adjusted" data. These seasonal factors represent a clearer picture of the seasonal pattern in the data. The last
   161 step is for motor fuel seasonal factors to be applied to the unadjusted data.
   162 For the seasonal factors introduced for January 2026, BLS adjusted 57 series using intervention analysis seasonal
   163 adjustment, including selected food and beverage items, motor fuels and vehicles.
   164 Revision of Seasonally Adjusted Indexes
   165 Seasonally adjusted data, including the U.S. city average all items index levels, are subject to revision for up to 5
   166 years after their original release. Every year, economists in the CPI calculate new seasonal factors for seasonally
   167 adjusted series and apply them to the last 5 years of data. Seasonally adjusted indexes beyond the last 5 years of
   168 data are considered to be final and not subject to revision. For January 2026, revised seasonal factors and seasonally
   169 adjusted indexes for 2021 to 2025 were calculated and published. For series which are directly adjusted using the
   170 Census X-13ARIMA-SEATS seasonal adjustment software, the seasonal factors for 2025 will be applied to data for 2026 to
   171 produce the seasonally adjusted 2026 indexes. Series which are indirectly seasonally adjusted by summing seasonally
   172 adjusted component series have seasonal factors which are derived and are therefore not available in advance.
   173 Determining Seasonal Status
   174 Each year the seasonal status of every series is reevaluated based upon certain statistical criteria. Using these
   175 criteria, BLS economists determine whether a series should change its status from "not seasonally adjusted" to
   176 "seasonally adjusted", or vice versa. If any of the 81 components of the U.S. city average all items index change
   177 their seasonal adjustment status from seasonally adjusted to not seasonally adjusted, not seasonally adjusted data
   178 will be used in the aggregation of the dependent series for the last 5 years, but the seasonally adjusted indexes
   179 before that period will not be changed. For 2026, 36 of the 81 components of the U.S. city average all items index are
   180 not seasonally adjusted.
   181 Contact Information
   182 For additional information about the CPI visit www.bls.gov/cpi or contact the CPI Information and Analysis Section at
   183 202-691-7000 or cpi_info@bls.gov.
   184 For additional information on seasonal adjustment in the CPI visit www.bls.gov/cpi/seasonal-adjustment/home.htm
   185 If you are deaf, hard of hearing, or have a speech disability, please dial 7-1-1 to access telecommunications relay
   186 services.
